 -0.7817613482475281], [" user", -1.2453577518463135]]}, {"t": " mode", "lp": -0.23529468476772308, "top": [[" mode", -0.23529468476772308], [" CCS", -2.161224603652954]]}, {"t": "**", "lp": -0.00041356292786076665, "top": [["**", -0.00041356292786076665], ["**.", -7.854671001434326]]}, {"t": " in", "lp": -0.4403466582298279, "top": [[" in", -0.4403466582298279], [" for", -1.0953798294067383]]}], "text": "Based on current technical documentation and driver behavior for Intel Arc GPUs (including the A770) under the Linux `xe` driver (or the newer `intel-mesa`/`i915` drivers), here is the breakdown:\n\n### 1. **CCS Mode 1 vs. Mode 2**\n\n**CCS (Constant Color Surface)** modes refer to how the GPU handles **color space conversions** and **gamma correction** during rendering, particularly when using **YUV** or **HDR** content.\n\n- **CCS Mode 1**:  \n  - **Standard sRGB / Linear workflow**.  \n  - Used for **SDR (Standard Dynamic Range)** content.  \n  - Applies standard gamma correction (typically 2.2 or sRGB curve).  \n  - **Default mode** for most desktop compositing and video playback.  \n  - **Use when**: You are displaying normal SDR content (e.g., YouTube videos, desktop UI, games in SDR).\n\n- **CCS Mode 2**:  \n  - **HDR (High Dynamic Range) workflow**.  \n  - Enables **linear color space** handling with **extended dynamic range**.  \n  - Requires **HDR-capable display** and **HDR metadata** (e.g., PQ10, HLG).  \n  - **Use when**: You are playing HDR video (e.g., Netflix HDR, YouTube HDR) or using HDR games, and your monitor supports HDR output.\n\n> **Key Difference**:  \n> - **Mode 1** = SDR (standard brightness, gamma-corrected).  \n> - **Mode 2** = HDR (linear, extended brightness, requires HDR display).\n\n---\n\n### 2. **When to Use CCS Mode 4?**\n\n**CCS Mode 4** is **not a standard public mode** in"}